<div class="article-body">

<p>I walked past my hydroponic setup for 2 weeks. The basil looked fine from above. But when I finally picked up the reservoir, the inside looked like a swamp. Green slime everywhere. The roots were coated in it. The pH had spiked to 7.8. My plants were slowly suffocating and I had no idea.</p>

<p>Here's the short version: algae in your hydroponic system is fixable. Use 3% hydrogen peroxide at 3ml per gallon. It kills algae in 24 hours without hurting your plants. But you need to catch it early. I almost lost my whole setup because I thought "a little green" was no big deal.</p>

<p>Full disclosure: I'm not a scientist. I'm just someone who let their system get disgusting and had to figure out how to fix it. If you caught it early, you'll be fine. If you're looking at a slime monster like I was, here's exactly what to do.</p>

<p>Here's the thing that surprised me most: <strong>the algae itself isn't the real problem.</strong> The real problem is what algae does to your water chemistry. When algae photosynthesizes in your reservoir, it consumes CO2 and drives the pH up. My pH went from 6.0 to 7.8 in 3 days. At 7.8, your plants can't absorb nutrients. They starve while sitting in fertilizer. That's what was happening to my basil. It wasn't the algae clogging the roots. It was the pH locking out all the nutrients.</p>

<h2 id="ignore">1. What Happens When You Ignore Algae</h2>

<p>I noticed a little green on my clay pebbles around day 10. I thought "it's fine, a little algae won't hurt." By day 14, the reservoir walls were slippery. By day 18, my basil looked pale and the leaves started yellowing. I checked the roots and they were brown and slimy.</p>

<p>Here's the timeline of what I missed:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> Green film on hydroton surface. Harmless at this stage. A quick wipe would have fixed it.</li>
  <li><strong>Day 14:</strong> Reservoir walls getting slippery. pH starting to drift. My basil looked fine above water. Below water? Roots were starting to suffocate.</li>
  <li><strong>Day 18:</strong> pH at 7.8. Roots brown. Leaves yellow. Plant growth stalled completely.</li>
</ul>

<p>I lost about 2 weeks of growth and almost killed a batch of basil that cost me $3 in seeds and a month of time. All because I didn't want to spend 10 minutes cleaning the reservoir.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Quick Math</div>
  <p>3ml of 3% hydrogen peroxide costs about $0.02. A $5 bottle lasts me 6 months of treatments. The alternative? Losing $30 worth of plants and starting over. The math does not favor ignoring it.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="why">2. Why Algae Grows in Hydroponic Systems</h2>

<p>Algae needs 3 things: light, water, and nutrients. Your hydroponic system has all 3. That's why algae always tries to take over. It's not a failure of your setup. It's just biology.</p>

<p>Here's what makes algae thrive in a hydroponic system:</p>

<p><strong>Light hitting the reservoir.</strong> This is the #1 cause. If light reaches your nutrient solution, algae will grow. Clear tubing, translucent reservoir lids, gaps around net pots. All of these are entry points for light. I use black reservoirs now. Problem solved.</p>

<p><strong>Warm water.</strong> Algae reproduces faster above 75°F (24°C). If your water temp is high, you'll fight algae constantly. My reservoir sits around 72°F normally. During a heat wave it hit 78°F and the algae exploded in 3 days.</p>

<p><strong>Stagnant water.</strong> Moving water discourages algae growth. Still water is a breeding ground. Make sure your air pump is running 24/7. I run a small air stone in my DWC bucket and it makes a visible difference.</p>

<p><strong>pH drifting alkaline.</strong> Algae prefers alkaline conditions above 7.0. When your pH drifts up, you're creating a algae-friendly environment. Keeping pH at 5.5-6.5 helps both your plants AND discourages algae. Double win.</p>

<p>Here's what nobody told me: <strong>algae feed on dead roots and decomposing plant matter.</strong> If you have dead roots sitting in your system, you're feeding the algae. Trim dead roots when you see them. Don't let them rot in the reservoir.</p>

<p>I thought I had it all figured out. I blocked the light, kept the water moving, checked pH twice a week. Then I bought a kit that made me want to quit.</p>

<h2 id="h2o2">3. The Hydrogen Peroxide Method That Works</h2>

<p>I tried scrubbing everything. I tried bleach. I tried emptying and refilling. Nothing worked long term. Then a Reddit thread mentioned hydrogen peroxide. I almost ignored it because it sounded too simple.</p>

<p>Here's the exact method I use now:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Get 3% hydrogen peroxide.</strong> Not the food grade 35% stuff. Not the 6% from a beauty supply store. Regular drugstore 3% hydrogen peroxide. A quart costs about $3 and lasts months.</li>
  <li><strong>Dose at 3ml per gallon</strong> of reservoir water. I use a syringe for accuracy. 3ml for a 1-gallon bucket. 12ml for a 4-gallon DWC. Add it directly to the reservoir.</li>
  <li><strong>Let it circulate for 24 hours.</strong> Keep your pump running. Don't add anything else. The hydrogen peroxide breaks down into water and oxygen. It kills algae cells without leaving residue.</li>
  <li><strong>After 24 hours, drain and refill</strong> with fresh nutrients. Your system should be clear.</li>
</ul>

<p>For roots that are already coated: gently rinse them under lukewarm water. Then pour diluted hydrogen peroxide (1 part H2O2 to 3 parts water) directly over the affected roots. Let it sit for 5 minutes. Rinse again. Your roots should look noticeably cleaner. I did this and my roots went from brown slime to nearly white in one treatment.</p>

<p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Hydrogen+Peroxide+3+percent+USP&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">3% hydrogen peroxide on Amazon</a>. About $3 for a quart. That's over 120 doses for a 1-gallon system.</p>

<p>One important warning: <strong>do not use hydrogen peroxide within 48 hours of adding beneficial bacteria</strong> like Hydroguard. H2O2 kills everything, good and bad. If you use beneficial bacteria, treat with H2O2 first, let it break down fully (24 hours), drain, then add fresh nutrients and beneficial bacteria after.</p>

<p>I use a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=hydroponic+pH+meter+digital&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">digital pH meter</a> to check my pH before and after treatment. The algae was driving my pH up, so after treatment, I had to re-adjust back to 5.8. Without a pH meter, you'd be guessing. It's the single most useful tool you can buy for under $15.</p>

<h2 id="flush">4. How to Do a Full System Flush</h2>

<p>If hydrogen peroxide doesn't clear it or the algae is severe like mine was, do a full system flush. Here's the step by step:</p>

<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Remove your plants. If you want to save them, rinse the roots gently under lukewarm water. Trim any brown or slimy roots. Suspend the plants in a jar of clean water while you work.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Drain the reservoir completely. Scrub all surfaces with hot water and dish soap. Pay attention to corners, pump housings, and tubing connections. I use a bottle brush for the tubing.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Mix a cleaning solution of 30ml 3% hydrogen peroxide per liter of water. Use this to scrub every surface. Let it sit for 15 minutes.</p>

<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> Rinse everything thoroughly with clean water. I rinse 3 times to make sure no hydrogen peroxide remains.</p>

<p><strong>Step 5:</strong> Refill with fresh nutrient solution. Adjust pH to 5.8. Add your plants back.</p>

<p>Full system flush takes about 45 minutes. But if you do it right, you won't need to do it again for months. I do this between grows now. It's part of my routine like changing the oil in my car.</p>

<p><a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Hydroguard+beneficial+bacteria+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Hydroguard on Amazon</a>. I add 10ml per gallon after each reservoir change. It contains beneficial bacteria that outcompete algae. I use it as a preventive now, not a cure.</p>

<h2 id="prevent">5. How to Stop Algae From Coming Back</h2>

<p>Here's what I changed after my algae disaster. My system has been clean for 3 months now.</p>

<p><strong>Block all light.</strong> I switched to black reservoirs. I wrapped clear tubing in electrical tape. I put aluminum foil around the net pots. Any gap gets blocked. Light is the #1 algae trigger. Remove light, remove algae.</p>

<p><strong>Check pH daily.</strong> I use a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=hydroponic+pH+meter+digital+Apera&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">digital pH meter</a> every morning. If pH is rising above 6.5, I investigate. A sudden pH rise is the earliest sign of algae. Catch it then and you avoid the whole mess.</p>

<p><strong>Add preventive treatments.</strong> I add 2ml of 3% hydrogen peroxide per gallon at every reservoir change. Not enough to harm anything. Just enough to keep algae from getting started. Or use grapefruit seed extract (5 drops per gallon). Both work.</p>

<p><strong>Keep water temps under 75°F.</strong> In summer, I use frozen water bottles in the reservoir. Simple trick. Rotate 2 frozen bottles throughout the day and the temp stays at 70-72°F.</p>

<p><strong>Maintain good water circulation.</strong> Make sure your <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=hydroponic+air+pump+with+air+stone&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">air pump and air stone</a> are running 24/7. Stagnant water is where algae colonizes first. Keep it moving.</p>

<p>I also upgraded my <a href="https://growlightdb.tabletopharvest.com" target="_blank">grow light</a> because the old one was heating up the reservoir. More light intensity meant I could raise the light higher, which kept the water cooler. The <a href="https://growlightdb.tabletopharvest.com" target="_blank">Grow Light DB</a> has full specs on low-heat options that won't cook your reservoir.</p>

